---
/**
 * website/src/pages/console.astro — the console page (website plan, Task W1.6).
 *
 * The spec's section 4 asks for *"Console downloads: per-OS download links (placeholder
 * until the console ships)"*, and the console is built and **not released**: `console/` holds
 * the application and the workflow that signs, notarises and staples its macOS artifacts, that
 * workflow creates the release as a draft the owner publishes, and `features.console_downloads`
 * is false until then. This page is that placeholder, and it is built so that the placeholder
 * is honest in the way the reconciliation asks for: it says what the console is, that it is not
 * released yet and what it will be, and it renders **no link and no button** a reader could
 * follow (finding 23, finding 25, D95).
 *
 * Where its facts come from, and why nothing here is typed into the markup:
 *
 *   - `website/content/downloads.json` — the console's own facts, read and validated by
 *     `src/lib/console-downloads.mjs`: what the console is, the sentence that it is not
 *     released, the properties the console's own decisions fix (read-only, no telemetry,
 *     its own artifact), the release its download URLs are built from, and one download
 *     per published asset. Every sentence below is rendered from that file, and
 *     `website/test/console.mjs` asserts that: a page that writes its own copy of a fact is
 *     the copy that drifts first (F37, Global Constraint 6).
 *   - `website/data/site.yaml` — the site's own facts, read through `loadSite()`: the
 *     feature flag `features.console_downloads`, which is the flag the Go boundary test
 *     holds the whole of `website/` to (`internal/cli/docs_website_boundary_test.go`,
 *     `TestWebsiteLinksNoConsoleWhileTheFlagIsOff`), and the `repository` every download
 *     URL is composed from.
 *
 * **The reader is shared, because two pages offer the console.** Getting Started's console
 * option renders the same artifact from the same module (the owner's changes spec of
 * 2026-10-18, item 6), so `src/lib/console-downloads.mjs` holds the two flags' rule, the
 * release's own shape and the URL derivation, and this page only renders what it returns.
 *
 * **The two flags are one fact, and the build refuses them disagreeing.** `site.yaml`'s
 * flag is what the repository-wide rule reads; `downloads.json`'s `available` is what the
 * section renders from. A build that rendered one state while the other said the opposite
 * is exactly the drift the placeholder exists to prevent, so the disagreement stops the
 * build, naming both files — the same shape `content.ts` gives the analytics flag and its
 * measurement id (W1.3 step 2).
 *
 * **And a URL may not exist under `website/` at all.** Every download URL is composed at
 * build time from the release's published names, so no file carries one — the state the Go
 * boundary test scans the whole site for while the flag is false (Global Constraint 9).
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import Downloads from '../components/Downloads.astro'
import { SITE_ROOT, loadSite } from '../content.ts'
import { loadConsoleDownloads } from '../lib/console-downloads.mjs'

// The whole generated layer is checked first — every page renders through the base layout,
// and this call is what a page does before it reads a fact of its own (W1.3 step 2).
const site = loadSite()
const facts = loadConsoleDownloads(SITE_ROOT, site.facts.features.consoleDownloads, site.facts.repository)
---

<Base title="Console" description={facts.what.text}>
  <article>
    <h1>{facts.name}</h1>
    <p>{facts.what.text}</p>

    {/* The downloads themselves: rendered from `downloads.json`, and rendering no link and
        no button at all while the console is unreleased (W1.6 step 1). */}
    <Downloads
      available={facts.available}
      note={facts.notReleased.text}
      released={facts.released}
      downloads={facts.downloads}
    />

    <section id="console-facts" aria-labelledby="console-facts-heading">
      <h2 id="console-facts-heading">Facts about the console</h2>
      <dl class="console-properties">
        {
          facts.properties.map((property) => (
            <div class="console-property">
              <dt>{property.title}</dt>
              <dd>{property.text}</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>
